from author_common import *
name='gensci_2_4'
S='Teacher checks local risk assessment and reagent labels before any practical. Use splash goggles, small quantities, labelled equipment and a stable tray; wash hands. No tasting, skin testing or direct smelling. Keep bleach out of all practical work. Never mix cleaners. If safe equipment is unavailable use the labelled evidence cards and record practical skill as not observed.'
I=[
item('G1','A cupboard of different substances','Fictional school-store case: labelled vinegar is sour, a bicarbonate solution is mildly basic, and one stored salt has become wet while washing-soda crystals have become powdery. These descriptions are a puzzle, not an instruction to taste or touch. Predict whether one explanation can account for all changes. During the unit, build a “Substance–evidence–explanation–safe use” chart.','Keep three questions distinct: aqueous acidity/basicity, chemical reactions, and water exchange between salts and air. Do not assume every salt is neutral or behaves identically. The school-store case is invented; observations during class must be recorded honestly, including no visible change.'),
item('G2','Indicator investigation','Materials per group: labelled wells with1mL each of food vinegar diluted1:4 with water, dilute sodium bicarbonate solution prepared by the teacher (1g in100mL water), and distilled water; universal-indicator paper and its manufacturer’s chart; separate droppers; goggles. Put one drop on separate indicator strips, compare with the chart and record an approximate pH and classification. Never return a dropper to another sample. Repeat one measurement. If strips are unavailable, analyse this explicitly illustrative evidence: vinegar pH3,bicarbonate pH8,water pH7.','Actual results depend on sample, water, concentration and indicator range; the illustrative values are not mandatory class results. For these aqueous samples near room temperature, pH below7 is acidic, above7 basic, about7 neutral. Colour alone needs the appropriate chart. Indicator estimates do not establish chemical identity or safety. Prepare samples before class; avoid coloured/flavoured vinegar that obscures colour.'),
item('G3','What an acid/base model explains','Evidence cards for reading, not experiments on bodies: A stomach acid supports digestion, but excessive acid may be neutralised by a suitable antacid base. Carbon dioxide made during respiration can dissolve in water and make it more acidic. Many chlorine bleaches are alkaline, but their colour-removing action is oxidation of coloured substances, not a general property of all bases. In a simple aqueous model acids increase H+ (more fully hydrated as H3O+) and soluble hydroxide bases supply OH−; not every base is a soluble hydroxide. Match each process to a role and state one limit of the model.','Explain the aqueous model without claiming all bases directly releaseOH− or all neutralisations give pH7. Antacid is an explanatory example, not an instruction to take medicine. No bleach, bodily fluids or breath-sharing experiments. Bleach/acid mixing can release hazardous chlorine gas. Teacher references: RSC microscale acid/base activities; University of Bristol/CDC hypochlorite explanations. Detailed sources are in sources/science_references.json.'),
item('G4','A base neutralises acid','Teacher demonstration after local risk assessment: use a tray,goggles,dropper,stirrer and universal-indicator paper. Put2mL of the prepared0.1mol/L hydrochloric acid in a small open well or beaker. Add prepared0.1mol/L sodium hydroxide in small drops,stirring and testing on fresh indicator paper. Stop at approximately neutral indication, then add a small excess to a separate matched trial. Learners record direction of pH change. Learners do not prepare or handle concentrated reagents. Fallback qualitative evidence card: initial acid pH1;near-balanced mixture pH7;excess alkali pH10 (illustrative,not measured).','For HCl andNaOH: hydrochloric acid+sodium hydroxide→sodium chloride+water. H++OH−→water is the particle-level simplification. Near-equal volumes are expected only because these particular solutions have equal concentration and1:1 reaction. Do not generalise equal volumes to every acid/base. No evaporation or heating. Teacher controls solutions and disposes of material under school policy; pH testing is required before any approved drain disposal.'),
item('G5','Acid and a carbonate','Class microscale activity only after teacher approval: each pair has an open cup on a tray with2mL diluted food vinegar,one small pre-measured pinch (about0.1g) sodium bicarbonate,a spatula and goggles. Add the powder slowly;observe without capping the container. Record bubbling. Never taste or test a gas with a flame. Alternative evidence: bubbles form when the two named reagents mix. Use the information “acid+carbonate→salt+water+carbon dioxide” to identify products; discuss why bubbles alone cannot identify a gas.','Bicarbonate (hydrogen carbonate) is used as an accessible related example: vinegar(acetic acid)+sodium hydrogen carbonate→sodium acetate+water+carbon dioxide. Explain this naming distinction. For a carbonate example on paper: hydrochloric acid+calcium carbonate→calcium chloride+water+carbon dioxide. Bubbling indicates gas evolution,not gas identity by itself. Allow time for cleanup;teacher supplies and collects all materials.'),
item('G6','Acid and a metal','Teacher-only microscale demonstration in a ventilated laboratory: put one small magnesium-ribbon piece (about2mm) in an open well on a tray;add2drops of prepared1mol/L hydrochloric acid. Teacher and observers wear goggles and keep a safe viewing distance. Observe bubbles and the metal becoming smaller. No flame,gas collection or sealed vessel. No nitric acid or other metal is substituted. If the teacher cannot provide the setting and approved risk assessment,use the observation card instead: magnesium gets smaller and a gas forms.','Magnesium+hydrochloric acid→magnesium chloride+hydrogen. The named reactants and established reaction model identify the gas;the bubbles alone do not. Magnesium is flammable and hydrogen is flammable:remove ignition sources. This is a narrowed adaptation of the RSC microscale acid-metal activity,not its full multi-acid procedure. Local teacher confirms reagent availability,cleanup and disposal. Evidence-card work does not demonstrate hands-on practical competence.'),
item('G7','Three salts exchange water with air','Illustrative comparison,not real measured data: each labelled salt starts at5.00g under its stated conditions,then is exposed to air. Salt A ends at5.30g and remains a solid;Salt B ends at6.20g and forms a solution;Salt C is a hydrated salt;it ends at4.20g and becomes powdery. For this comparison the only mass transfer is water. Calculate mass change and classify each behaviour. What evidence would you need before applying this explanation to an unknown powder?','A takes up0.30g water:hygroscopic.B takes up1.20g and dissolves:deliquescent(a special hygroscopic behaviour).C loses0.80g water:efflorescent if the lost water is water of crystallisation. These are conditioned illustrative cases,not substance-identification tests. Appearance alone cannot establish identity,chemical safety or purity. Humidity and temperature affect whether and how fast changes occur.'),
item('G8','Prepare a fair exposure comparison','Teacher demonstration or prepared samples: clearly labelled small samples of calcium chloride and sodium carbonate decahydrate(washing soda),kept in separate trays out of reach. Teacher prepares an exposed and a covered comparison of each in advance,records time and room conditions,and photographs or weighs them. Learners use observations supplied by the teacher;no handling or heating. Decide which comparisons are fair and whether the evidence supports water gain or loss. If samples or balances are unavailable,use G7 only and mark this as data interpretation.','Calcium chloride can absorb enough atmospheric water to form solution;washing-soda crystals can lose water of crystallisation. Outcomes depend on air conditions,so never promise a visible result within40minutes. The teacher checks supplier safety data and uses goggles;avoid dust,skin contact and heat released when calcium chloride contacts water. Do not add water or mix the salts. Keep prepared samples away from food;dispose under school policy. This lesson assesses planning and interpretation,not an assured same-lesson transformation.'),
item('G9','Useful salts and responsible use','Read the application cards: food-grade sodium chloride is used in food;fertiliser salts supply nutrients to crops;some medicines contain salts;washing soda is used in laundry. Excess fertiliser nutrients reaching water can contribute to eutrophication. Excess dietary sodium is associated with raised blood pressure,but sodium also has necessary body functions. Create a classroom information poster with(a)one useful application,(b)one environmental or health concern,(c)one safe-use action. Include a separate “never mix cleaners” message. Where a device and an approved offline or online source are available,find one supporting statement and record its source;otherwise use the printed teacher reference and mark digital research as not observed. This is school-based education,no medical advice or actual community distribution.','Keep “salt” as a chemical class distinct from only table salt. Food-grade and dose/context matter;not all salts are edible. For health use WHO sodium-reduction evidence;give no individual intake target or treatment. Accept reading labels,avoiding unnecessary added salt,preventing fertiliser runoff or following trained advice as context-appropriate messages. Teacher checks wording before any later public sharing. Outcome f is assessed through a reasoned action,not agreement with a personal opinion.'),
item('G10','Independent assessment evidence','All numbers are illustrative. Aqueous solutions P,Q,R have approximate indicator pH3,8,7 near room temperature. A5.00g sample ends at5.80g and forms a solution after exposure to air;another5.00g hydrated sample ends at4.50g and becomes powdery,with water the only exchanged material. A laboratory record names dilute hydrochloric acid and magnesium;another names hydrochloric acid and calcium carbonate. Use these data with the unit’s reaction and safe-use models.','Full key is in the Final Explanation teacher document. This assessment uses evidence,not additional laboratory work. Responses must distinguish observations from inferred products and must not identify pH as a general safety test.')]
checks=[check('G7A','5.30-5',.30),check('G7B','6.20-5',1.20),check('G7C','4.20-5',-.80),check('G10gain','5.80-5',.8),check('G10loss','4.50-5',-.5)]
def make(n,title,outs,ids,knowledge,task,explanation,evidence,model,safety=S):
 return lesson(n,title,outs,ids,'Use observations and a stated model to explain a substance’s behaviour.',knowledge,'Record evidence,distinguish an observation from an inference,and justify a classification.','How can evidence distinguish a chemical reaction from water exchange with air?',task,[
 (f'Read {ids[0]} and make an individual prediction with a reason.','Allow quiet thinking,then a paired comparison;record disagreements before giving the explanation.','Every learner makes a testable or evidence-linked prediction.'),
 (task,'Use the named companion card and its preparation/safety conditions. Assign observer,recorder,materials monitor and reporter. If a practical is replaced by data,write “data interpretation” on the record.',evidence),
 (explanation,'Ask “What did you observe,and what are you inferring?” Check two individual explanations before recording the model.',evidence),
 ('Revisit the school-store questions and mark what the evidence answers.','Require a card or class-observation reference;leave unsupported claims open.','Learners distinguish a supported conclusion from a remaining question.'),
 (model,'Annotate the shared Substance–evidence–explanation–safe use chart without erasing the initial prediction.','Collect a short claim with evidence and a stated limit.')],['The named card or class practical provides the evidence.',knowledge,model],safety=safety)
L=[make(1,'Classify safe aqueous samples',['a'],['G1','G2'],'Universal-indicator colour and a chart estimate aqueous pH;for these samples pH below7 is acidic and above7 basic.','Carry out G2 with the teacher-prepared safe samples or analyse its labelled illustrative results. Record actual colours,pH estimates and a repeat.','Compare the evidence with initial cupboard predictions;classification is not proof of safety or chemical identity.','Each learner gives a chart-based classification for all three samples.','Begin the shared chart with a separate pH classification column.'),
make(2,'Acids and bases in familiar processes',['b'],['G3'],'An aqueous ion model helps explain acidity and neutralisation;bleaching also involves oxidation.','Read G3 and sort the roles in digestion,respiration,antacid neutralisation and bleaching.','Explain H+ andOH− at an introductory level,then identify where a pH-only explanation is insufficient.','Each learner correctly explains two biological/chemical roles and rejects “all bases bleach”.','Add a cause column that distinguishes pH from a specific chemical action.',safety='Reading only. No medicines are taken,no bodily fluids used,and no bleach is present. Never mix household cleaners.'),
make(3,'Observe neutralisation',['b','c'],['G4'],'For the named acid and hydroxide,neutralisation produces a salt and water.','Observe the teacher’s G4 demonstration;record pH direction before,near and beyond the endpoint. Use evidence data if laboratory conditions are unavailable.','Write the named word equation and explain the1:1 aqueous model without generalising equal volumes.','Each learner names sodium chloride and water and predicts the direction after excess alkali.','Add a reaction branch:acid+hydroxide base→salt+water.'),
make(4,'Explain bubbling with a carbonate',['c'],['G5'],'An acid with a carbonate or hydrogen carbonate can produce salt,water and carbon dioxide.','Complete the approved G5 microscale activity,including setup,observation and cleanup;otherwise interpret its observation card.','Distinguish bicarbonate from carbonate and explain why gas identity needs more than bubbles alone.','Each learner names three products for the named paper carbonate example.','Add a carbonate branch and separate observation from established reaction knowledge.'),
make(5,'Explain an acid–metal reaction',['c'],['G6'],'Magnesium with dilute hydrochloric acid produces magnesium chloride and hydrogen.','Observe the teacher-only G6 demonstration or use its recorded observation card;do not handle reagents as learners.','Name reactants and products and compare the gas with the carbonate reaction.','Each learner distinguishes hydrogen here from carbon dioxide in G5.','Add a separate acid–metal branch;no flame test is needed.'),
make(6,'Classify water gain and loss',['d'],['G7'],'Hygroscopic salts take up moisture;deliquescent salts take up enough to dissolve;efflorescent hydrated salts lose water of crystallisation.','Calculate and classify the three G7 cases;label data as illustrative and state the water-only assumption.','Explain that deliquescence is a hygroscopic behaviour,and a negative mass change does not itself prove water of crystallisation without the stated model.','A:+0.30g,hygroscopic;B:+1.20g,deliquescent;C:−0.80g,efflorescent under the stated conditions.','Add a water-exchange branch distinct from the acid/base reaction branches.',safety='Paper data only;do not handle or taste unknown powders.'),
make(7,'Plan a fair salt-exposure comparison',['d'],['G7','G8'],'Humidity,temperature,exposure and controls affect evidence of water exchange.','Inspect prepared G8 observations if available;design an exposed-versus-covered comparison and identify controlled variables.','Explain why a40-minute lesson may not show a change and why unchanged material is still a legitimate observation.','Each learner identifies a control and one limitation;do not award hands-on credit for card analysis.','Add storage conditions and an evidence limitation to the water-exchange model.'),
make(8,'Use salts without ignoring consequences',['e','f'],['G9'],'Salts have varied uses;proper use depends on identity,quantity and environmental context.','Read G9 application cards and make a use–benefit–risk–action table for three applications.','Explain the nutrient/runoff example and the distinction between a useful food salt and all salts being safe to eat.','Each learner gives a correct application and a justified risk-reduction action.','Extend the shared chart with benefits and responsible-use actions.',safety='Reading and discussion only. No eating,tasting,fertiliser handling or medical advice.'),
make(9,'Explain the cupboard and communicate safely',['a','b','c','d','e','f'],['G1','G9'],'A complete explanation may need separate pH,reaction and water-exchange models.','Create the G9 poster and apply the shared chart to the fictional G1 store case. Exchange posters for checking.','Explain why one claim such as “it is basic” cannot account for every change in the case.','Peer checker can trace each claim to a card or observation;teacher checks safety/health wording.','Complete the Substance–evidence–explanation–safe use chart with limits visible.',safety='Classroom poster only;teacher checks before any public sharing.No chemical mixtures or dietary prescriptions.')]
L.append(lesson(10,'Independent evidence-based explanation',['a','b','c','d','e','f'],['G10'],'Demonstrate the six outcomes using new evidence.','Classify,explain and apply the unit’s chemical and water-exchange models.','Separate observation,inference and safe-use reasoning.','Which model is justified by the evidence?','Learners independently interpret a fresh evidence card and answer the five assessment sections.',[
('Read G10 and the student assessment.','Allow4 minutes to clarify words and access needs without supplying explanations.','Check all learners can read the pH and mass values.'),
('Complete the assessment independently.','Allow30 minutes.No live practical.No teacher key is supplied.','Collect original answers and mark by outcome.'),
('Write a private reflection about one inference.','Use2 minutes;discuss full solutions in a later feedback session.','Record whether evidence and inference are distinguished.'),
('Submit one question for teacher feedback.','Use1 minute to collect questions.','Identify a topic needing reteaching.'),
('Write one responsible-use action with a reason.','Allow3 minutes for transfer reflection.','Use this and G9 as evidence for outcome f.')],['Independent responses apply the models to unfamiliar data.','pH,reaction products and salt-air behaviour require different evidence.','Responsible use combines an explanation with an appropriate action.'],safety='Paper assessment only;no tasting,chemical handling or personal health advice.',minutes=[4,30,2,1,3]))
m=dict(subject='General Science',substrand_id='2.4',topic='Acids Bases and Salts',folder='General_Science',prefix='Codex_General_Science_Acids_Bases_and_Salts',unit=dict(strand='2.0 Matter and Chemical Reactions',substrand='Sub-Strand 2.4: Acids, Bases and Salts',content='Acids and bases;Reactions of acids and bases;Salts.',coreCompetencies='Communication and collaboration;Self-efficacy;Digital literacy. Learners communicate evidence,prepare a safe-use poster and inspect attributed information in print or digital form.',values='Responsibility;Respect. Learners handle evidence honestly and listen to different explanations.',pcis='Socio-economic and environmental education;Safety and security;Learner support programme(peer education).',sep='Planning comparisons,observing,using simple models and arguing from evidence.',careers='Laboratory support,agriculture,food processing and environmental monitoring.',focus='Explain aqueous acidity,selected reactions and salt-air behaviour with distinct models and safe-use evidence.',phenomenon='A fictional school store contains acidic vinegar,a mildly basic bicarbonate solution,a salt that becomes wet and washing-soda crystals that become powdery. Can one explanation account for all of them?',supportingPhenomena='Microscale indicator/carbonate activities,teacher-controlled acid/base and acid/metal demonstrations,and labelled illustrative salt-exposure data.',drivingQuestion='Which evidence helps us explain and use these substances responsibly? KICD: Why are salts important in day-to-day life? How do salts behave when exposed to air?',storylineThread='pH classification -> biological/chemical roles -> neutralisation -> carbonate -> metal -> salt-water exchange -> fair comparison -> uses/risks -> communication -> assessment.'),lessons=L,assessment=dict(subjectLabel='ACIDS, BASES AND SALTS',instructions='Time:30 minutes within Lesson10.Use G10.Work independently;give evidence for each explanation.No experiments are performed.All numerical data are illustrative.',sections=[dict(title='1. Classify and explain an indicator result',prompt='Classify P,Q andR in G10.State one limitation of using this result to decide whether a substance is safe.',exemplar='P acidic,Q basic,R approximately neutral for these aqueous samples near room temperature. pH does not identify a substance or establish its safety;neutral substances can still be harmful.'),dict(title='2. A role and a limit',prompt='Explain one role of acid in digestion and why “all bases bleach” is not a justified rule.',exemplar='Stomach acid supports digestion,including providing acidic conditions for relevant digestive processes. Bleaching by hypochlorite involves oxidation of coloured substances;being a base does not by itself confer bleaching action. No medicine-use instruction is implied.'),dict(title='3. Identify reaction products',prompt='For each named reaction in G10,give the products. Also state the products of hydrochloric acid reacting with sodium hydroxide. Explain why bubbles alone are insufficient to identify a gas.',exemplar='HCl+magnesium→magnesium chloride+hydrogen. HCl+calcium carbonate→calcium chloride+water+carbon dioxide. HCl+sodium hydroxide→sodium chloride+water. Gas identity follows from the named reaction and supporting chemical knowledge;different gases can cause bubbles.'),dict(title='4. Explain salt-air behaviour',prompt='Calculate both mass changes in G10.Classify each behaviour under the stated assumptions.Explain the relation between deliquescence and hygroscopy.',exemplar='First change=5.80−5.00=+0.80g;water uptake with solution formation is deliquescence.Second=4.50−5.00=−0.50g;loss of water of crystallisation from the stated hydrate is efflorescence.Deliquescence is a hygroscopic behaviour with sufficient uptake to form solution;not every observed water uptake reaches that state.'),dict(title='5. Responsible application',prompt='Describe one useful salt application,one related health or environmental concern,and one justified action.Include one safe rule about household cleaners.',exemplar='Example:fertiliser salts provide crop nutrients;runoff can contribute to eutrophication;use appropriate quantities and prevent runoff into water.Accept another correct application with a linked concern/action.Never mix cleaners,especially bleach with acids.No personal medical prescription is required.')],rubric=rubric(['Classify aqueous samples using indicator evidence','Explain roles and identify products of the named reactions','Classify salt-air behaviour using mass and state evidence','Explain an application and communicate responsible use'])))
publish(name,m,I,checks,'Ten40-minute lessons separate indicator work,three reaction contexts,salt-air evidence,application and communication.The final assessment has its own lesson.No visible official allocation is inferred.',[dict(task='G2 and assessment1',outcomes=['a']),dict(task='G3 and assessment2',outcomes=['b']),dict(task='G4/G5/G6 and assessment3',outcomes=['c']),dict(task='G7/G8 and assessment4',outcomes=['d']),dict(task='G9 and assessment5',outcomes=['e','f'])])
